"""Category 8 top-up (3): README-vs-code discrepancies, before/after comparisons, roadmap Q&A, and 'what would the assistant say' cases."""
from lib import *

CAT = "nexus_knowledge"
R = seeded("nexus3")
out = []

QA = [
 ("The README says NEXUS has Ollama support. Should I rely on that?", "I wouldn't. The README lists Ollama under Phase 12, but the code audit found no Ollama references in the backend, the requirements file says Ollama is deliberately absent, and the Phase 12 voice document says NEXUS runs no language model. Treat the README line as not implemented."),
 ("Where can I read what NEXUS can really do, if the README overstates it?", "The most reliable sources are the code itself and the docs under docs/, especially docs/phase-12-voice.md and the specifications for phases 10 and 11. The README is a good overview of intent, but it describes some AI features (Ollama, web intelligence) more broadly than the audited code supports."),
 ("What's different between the README and reality for NEXUS's AI?", "The README mentions a Voice + Ollama phase and Web / Internet Intelligence. In the code, voice is browser-based speech recognition and synthesis feeding an intent classifier, there's no Ollama integration, no web client, and no generative model. The one executing path is propose and confirm."),
 ("Summarise NEXUS's AI today in two sentences.", "NEXUS's AI is a DeBERTa-based intent classifier that routes a sentence to the right module, plus hand-written parsers that turn some sentences into proposals you must confirm. It has no generative language model, no chat, and no generic tool system yet."),
 ("Summarise the planned NEXUS AI in two sentences.", "The plan is to fine-tune Qwen3-8B as the single generative model so NEXUS can converse, reason and decide when to use tools, with NEXUS services and a permissioned tool layer doing the real work. None of this is implemented yet, and it will only be integrated after evaluation passes."),
 ("What changes for the user once Qwen is integrated?", "In the plan, the assistant would answer questions, explain code, plan with you and request tools, instead of only naming which module a request belongs to. Writes would still go through confirmation. That's a plan, not today's behaviour."),
 ("What stays the same once Qwen is integrated?", "The application's accounts and permissions, REST services, planner, analytics, risk engine and the propose-and-confirm safety principle should stay. Qwen would be a new reasoning layer calling the same services through controlled tools."),
 ("What would the assistant say if I asked it today to explain my Python error?", "Today NEXUS would classify it as code_assist and answer generation_unavailable, because it has no language model. A model-based assistant (the Qwen plan) is what is meant to handle it."),
 ("What would the planned assistant do if I ask it to delete a project?", "In the plan, it would propose the deletion with the exact project named, flag it as destructive and wait for your confirmation before any service runs. Without a clear target it would ask which project you mean."),
 ("What's the safest way to describe NEXUS to someone new?", "NEXUS is a personal planning and intelligence platform with tasks, projects, a planner, knowledge, analytics, risks, developer, learning and career modules. Its AI today routes your requests to those modules; a conversational AI assistant is planned but not built."),
 ("How do I phrase NEXUS's voice feature accurately?", "Say that you can speak a request using your browser's speech recognition, and NEXUS routes the text to the right area and reads a short status back. Mention that in Chrome and Edge the audio goes to the browser vendor, and that it doesn't answer questions yet."),
 ("Is NEXUS an AI agent?", "Not today. An agent would plan, call tools and verify results. NEXUS has a classifier and a propose-and-confirm action flow, which is much narrower. The Qwen roadmap aims toward agent-like behaviour in later phases."),
 ("Does NEXUS use cloud AI APIs?", "No. The project's rule has been to avoid paid or cloud AI APIs: the classifier runs locally, speech comes from the browser, and the Qwen plan uses open weights trained on a free Kaggle GPU."),
 ("Why does the Qwen plan use free resources?", "The project prefers a zero-cost or very low-cost workflow: open-source models, Kaggle's free GPU for training, local inference where practical and no paid APIs unless explicitly approved later."),
 ("What was the Phase 1 audit for?", "It was a read-only review that compared the README with the real code, documented the architecture, identified where a language model could attach later and defined the training approach, without modifying the application."),
 ("What did Phase 1 conclude about the existing dataset scripts?", "That about twenty untracked scripts at the repository root and in scripts/ were from an earlier dataset attempt pointing at a folder that doesn't exist, and that they should not be trusted or modified. The new dataset work lives in an isolated workspace."),
 ("How are the Qwen phases gated?", "Each phase must pass its gate before the next starts: specification must be consistent, the dataset must pass validation, tool schemas and traces must validate, a training dry run must succeed, training must produce a loadable adapter, and evaluation must show useful agent behaviour. Integration happens only after that."),
 ("What's the point of an evaluation gate before integration?", "It prevents integrating a model that looks fine by training loss but hallucinates actions, ignores permissions or loses general ability. If the model fails, the plan is to fix the data rather than ship it."),
 ("Why not integrate Qwen immediately?", "Because an untested model with tools could claim actions it didn't perform or act unsafely. The roadmap trains and evaluates it in isolation first, and the runtime enforces permissions regardless of what the model says."),
 ("Who makes the final decision to act in NEXUS?", "The user and the runtime. The model can propose; the permission layer decides what's allowed; writes need confirmation. A model's text never executes anything by itself."),
 ("Could NEXUS's classifier help the future assistant?", "Possibly, as a cheap pre-router or a guard to catch unsupported requests. The roadmap leaves that decision until after Qwen is evaluated."),
 ("What does 'single generative model' mean in this project?", "It means only one language model, Qwen3-8B, generates text; there is no second LLM and no model-routing between LLMs. Non-generative components such as the DeBERTa classifier are not counted."),
 ("Why was a second model rejected before?", "The earlier rule kept NEXUS explainable and measurable around one artefact, and each additional model adds weights to train, serve and trust. Qwen will legitimately change the rule, so the docs and tests that assert 'no language model' will need deliberate updates."),
 ("Is the dataset specific to NEXUS only?", "No. It deliberately mixes general conversation, knowledge, reasoning, programming, maths and instruction following with NEXUS and tool-awareness behaviour, so the model stays broadly useful instead of becoming a NEXUS-only bot."),
 ("What does the dataset teach about tools?", "When a request needs a tool and when it doesn't, to read the runtime's tool list from the system message, to propose a call without claiming results, to ask for clarification, to report failures honestly, and to say a capability is unavailable when it is."),
 ("Does the dataset give Qwen real tool access?", "No. Training data teaches behaviour, not capability. Real access to the web, files or NEXUS must come from the runtime tool layer, which does not exist yet."),
 ("Which hardware does the project have?", "A Windows 11 laptop with about 15.6 GB RAM, an RTX 3050 laptop GPU with roughly 4 GB, an Intel Iris Xe GPU and around 160 GB of free disk space. Training an 8B model needs Kaggle; local work is limited to data preparation and small dry runs."),
 ("Can the RTX 3050 run Qwen3-8B?", "Not comfortably. A 4-bit 8B model is around 5 to 6 GB, more than the 4 GB of GPU memory, so it would need CPU offloading and would be slow. This should be measured in the preparation phase before committing to local inference."),
 ("What did the Phase 10 lesson teach about data?", "That template-generated data can score very high in-distribution and still generalise poorly: the classifier hit about 0.974 on synthetic test data and 75% on hand-written requests. So the Qwen dataset stresses diverse wording, hand-written evaluation and leakage checks."),
 ("What happens when someone asks NEXUS something out of scope?", "The classifier may label it out_of_scope and NEXUS says it's outside what it can route. Note the classifier sometimes misfires, for example sending conversational text to risk_query."),
 ("Is the voice assistant private?", "Text-to-speech is local, but speech recognition in Chrome and Edge sends your audio to the browser vendor. NEXUS discloses this on the assistant page. Typing is always available."),
 ("What would need to exist for NEXUS to browse the web?", "A web tool in the runtime (search and fetch with size and domain limits), a permission policy for it, an executor that returns real results, and a model trained to propose it and report only returned results. None of that exists today."),
 ("What would need to exist for NEXUS to edit files?", "A file tool restricted to allowlisted folders, confirmation for writes and deletions, an executor with audit logging, and a model that proposes edits without claiming they happened. Nothing like that exists today."),
 ("How is NEXUS different from a chatbot?", "A chatbot centres on conversation. NEXUS is a structured application, with your projects, tasks and data, where AI is meant to help decide and act in a controlled way. Today it has no chat, and the plan adds conversation on top of the application."),
]
FR = ["{q}", "Quick check: {ql}", "Can you answer this about NEXUS? {q}"]
for i, (q, a) in enumerate(QA):
    for k in range(3):
        u = FR[k].format(q=q, ql=q[0].lower() + q[1:])
        out.append(mk(CAT, "project_qa", [("user", u), ("assistant", a)], f"n3_{i}", "medium", "authored" if k == 0 else "composed", R.choice(SYS_NEXUS_KNOW)))

# multi-turn NEXUS dialogs
D = [
 [("user", "What AI does NEXUS have?"), ("assistant", "One model: a DeBERTa intent classifier that routes a sentence to the right module. There's no generative model."), ("user", "So I can't ask it questions?"), ("assistant", "Not for written answers. You can ask, and it will tell you which area your request belongs to, or propose an action to confirm, but it can't compose an answer."), ("user", "And that changes with Qwen?"), ("assistant", "That's the plan: Qwen3-8B would be the single generative model for conversation and reasoning. It's not trained or integrated yet, so today nothing changes.")],
 [("user", "Does NEXUS support Ollama?"), ("assistant", "The README says so, but the audit found no Ollama code, so I'd treat it as not implemented."), ("user", "Then how would a local model run?"), ("assistant", "The integration plan suggests a separate local inference process behind an OpenAI-style interface, with the backend calling it, so the existing pinned CPU torch stack stays untouched."), ("user", "Is that built?"), ("assistant", "No. It's a plan for the integration phase, which only starts after the model passes evaluation.")],
 [("user", "Can I use my voice to create a task?"), ("assistant", "Partly. Your browser transcribes your speech, NEXUS classifies the text and can propose a task, but you still review and confirm before it's created."), ("user", "Does my voice go to the cloud?"), ("assistant", "In Chrome and Edge, yes: the audio goes to the browser vendor for transcription. NEXUS itself only receives the text, and Firefox has no speech recognition."), ("user", "Okay, I'll type it then.")  , ("assistant", "That works exactly the same way. The typed field takes the same route to the classifier, so nothing is lost.")],
 [("user", "What's the Qwen dataset phase?"), ("assistant", "Phase 2 of the roadmap: building and validating a training dataset of over ten thousand chat-format examples. It doesn't train anything or change NEXUS."), ("user", "What's in it?"), ("assistant", "Twelve categories: general conversation, knowledge, reasoning, programming, maths, instruction following, assistant behaviour, NEXUS knowledge, tool-aware reasoning, capability boundaries, multi-turn context and safety."), ("user", "Why include general stuff?"), ("assistant", "So the model stays broadly useful rather than becoming a NEXUS-only bot. The NEXUS and tool behaviour is added on top of general ability.")],
 [("user", "Will the assistant be able to delete files?"), ("assistant", "Only if a file tool is built, restricted to allowlisted folders and gated by confirmation. Nothing like that exists today."), ("user", "What if the model decides to delete something by itself?"), ("assistant", "In the plan, the model can only propose a call. The runtime validates the arguments, checks permissions and requires your confirmation for destructive actions, so the model's decision alone never executes anything."), ("user", "Good. And who checks the model behaves?"), ("assistant", "The evaluation phase: it tests tool selection, hallucinated execution, clarification and permission behaviour against the base model before any integration.")],
 [("user", "Is the classifier accurate?"), ("assistant", "It scored about 0.974 on its synthetic test set, but only 42 of 56 (75%) on hand-written natural-language requests."), ("user", "Why the gap?"), ("assistant", "The test data was template-generated, so it resembled the training data closely. Real phrasing is messier, and some misses collapsed into risk_query."), ("user", "Does the confidence threshold protect me?"), ("assistant", "Partly. The 0.90 threshold stops low-confidence routing, but some wrong predictions scored above it, so it's a guard, not a guarantee.")],
]
for i, d in enumerate(D):
    for rep in range(2):
        out.append(mk(CAT, "multi_turn_dialog", d, f"n3d_{i}", "medium", "authored", R.choice(SYS_NEXUS_KNOW) if rep == 0 else None, {"turns": len([t for t in d if t[0] == 'user'])}))

save("nexus3", out)
